@page rh="EZRA · 3 OF 5 · CHAPTERS 9 AND 10" m="EZRA 9; EZRA 10; NEH 13.23–30; DEUT 7.1–4; SOUTHWOOD, ETHNICITY AND THE MIXED MARRIAGE CRISIS; ESKENAZI, IN AN AGE OF PROSE"
note: The expulsion of women and their children from a community by collective decision, and the ethical dispute over how to describe it. You can work this page later, or not at all, and the spread still functions.
# The boundary, and who paid for it
rec:
What happens | Officials report that men of the community, priests and Levites among them, have married women of the surrounding peoples. Ezra tears his clothes and prays a long confession in the first person plural. A proposal is made to send away the wives and the children born to them. An assembly agrees, a commission examines cases household by household, and a list of names is recorded.
The children | The children are named in the proposal alongside the wives. INTERPRETATION: Here a boundary about identity becomes a decision about particular children.
The silences | No woman speaks, and none is named. Where they went, who took them in, whether they had families to return to and what happened to the children aren't recorded. A few men object (10.15), and the text doesn't explain why.
What "foreign" means | CONTESTED: Some read an ethnic boundary, some a religious one, and some a fight over land and inheritance between returnees and people who never left. Others read the list of peoples as old legal language more than a description of the actual women. The stakes are high, and the text doesn't settle it.
Scale | The recorded list is a little over a hundred men. CONTESTED: Whether the action was carried out fully, partly, or is an idealized account of a policy. The parallel scene in Nehemiah 13 suggests it didn't stay settled.
box: On the phrase this companion won't use
INTERPRETATION: "Ethnic cleansing with a liturgy" carries a modern legal category into a text whose scale, mechanism and definition of the group are all disputed. It also flattens a real, serious ethical problem into a slogan, which makes it easier to dismiss. The ethical dispute stays on this page in full. A forceful wording of the objection doesn't get treated as accurate for being forceful.
/end
prompt: Write one of the women. Name her. Give her the sentence the text didn't. | fill

@page rh="EZRA · 4 OF 5 · THE PAPERWORK · WRITING" m="WRITING; EZRA 4.11–16; EZRA 5.7–17; EZRA 7.12–26; TKC: DOM"
# Write the Empire's file
Ezra is a book that quotes its own correspondence. Draft the Empire's documents on the same model, a letter of complaint, a search of the archive and a decree that settles it.
prompt: 1 · The letter: who writes to the capital to stop a rebuilding, and what danger they name | fill
prompt: 2 · The archive search: what's found, and who gets to read it | fill
prompt: 3 · The decree: its opening words, its funding line and its penalty clause | fill

